Initial Reference Architecture for Unstructured Data for AI (A1)

1. Purpose

This document provides the initial draft reference architecture for the Unstructured Data for AI workstream.

Its purpose is to define a platform-agnostic, enterprise-level architecture view for how heterogeneous unstructured and semi-structured content can be:
	•	onboarded from multiple source domains
	•	prepared and normalised into a governed content form
	•	processed through deterministic and AI-enabled workflows
	•	integrated with shared AI services in a controlled manner
	•	persisted across role-specific storage services
	•	exposed for retrieval, analytics, and downstream business use

This is an initial logical reference architecture. It is intended to establish the baseline architectural direction for the workstream and support further refinement, stakeholder alignment, and gap assessment.

⸻

2. Scope

The A1 architecture is intended to support a broad range of unstructured and semi-structured source types, including but not limited to:
	•	internal documents and knowledge content
	•	workflow, case, and ticket-based content
	•	communication and collaboration content
	•	audio, transcripts, and interaction media
	•	external and third-party content
	•	enterprise file, storage, and repository sources

The architecture is designed to support a range of downstream patterns, including:
	•	retrieval and search
	•	RAG and assistant experiences
	•	stateful agent workflows
	•	knowledge graph construction
	•	structured downstream data products
	•	enterprise application integration
	•	analytics and reporting use cases

It is intentionally platform-agnostic and does not assume a single cloud, product, or implementation topology.

⸻

3. Architecture Intent

The architecture is intended to solve a common enterprise problem:

unstructured data is already being used in multiple ways across AI use cases, but current delivery patterns are fragmented, use-case-led, and not yet aligned to a shared architecture model.

The A1 reference architecture is therefore designed to provide:
	•	a common lifecycle model for unstructured content
	•	a shared capability view across ingestion, preparation, processing, storage, and retrieval
	•	a clear place for security, governance, lineage, and policy controls
	•	a reusable pattern that can support different source types and use cases without being tied to one project

This architecture should be read as a logical enterprise capability model, not as a detailed deployment diagram or project-specific implementation.

⸻

4. Architectural Principles

The A1 architecture is based on the following principles:

4.1 Source-agnostic by design

The architecture must support heterogeneous source domains without assuming one dominant source pattern.

4.2 Modality-aware processing

Documents, audio, chat, email, tickets, and external content require different preparation paths and should not be forced through a single generic processing pattern.

4.3 Canonical content before downstream AI use

Content should be normalised, enriched, classified, and governed before it is indexed, embedded, graphed, or exposed for retrieval.

4.4 Shared controls across the full lifecycle

Identity, policy, lineage, DLP, encryption, observability, and retention must apply across ingestion, processing, storage, and retrieval.

4.5 Multiple persistence models, clear roles

Raw, processed, metadata, analytical, vector, graph, and session-state persistence each serve distinct purposes and should not be collapsed into a single storage model.

4.6 Governed AI and tool interaction

AI runtimes should access tools, services, and models through governed integration patterns rather than direct unmanaged connections.

4.7 Support deterministic and agentic execution

The architecture must support both deterministic workflows and stateful agent patterns, with agentic execution used where complexity justifies it.

4.8 Retrieval is a governed capability

Retrieval must include entitlement, policy, provenance, and ranking controls, not just technical search capability.

