5. Layered Architecture Overview

The A1 diagram is structured as a left-to-right lifecycle across the following layers:
	1.	Source Domains
	2.	Ingestion & Onboarding
	3.	Content Preparation & Normalisation
	4.	Orchestration & Semantic Processing
	5.	Governed Integration & AI Services
	6.	Persistence Services
	7.	Retrieval & Consumption
	8.	Cross-Cutting Security, Governance & Operations

Each layer has a distinct architectural purpose.

⸻

6. Layer-by-Layer Description

6.1 Source Domains

This layer represents the domains in which content originates.

It includes broad source classes rather than individual products so that the architecture remains stable even as implementation technologies change.

Typical source domains include:
	•	internal knowledge and document sources
	•	operational workflow and case sources
	•	communication and collaboration sources
	•	media and interaction sources
	•	external and third-party content sources
	•	enterprise storage and repository sources

This layer makes it explicit that the platform must support heterogeneous content, not just documents.

⸻

6.2 Ingestion & Onboarding

This layer provides the controlled path by which source content enters the platform.

It typically includes:
	•	source registration and ownership setup
	•	access configuration and source entitlement setup
	•	connector, API, batch, event, stream, or crawler-based intake
	•	landing and raw intake handling
	•	validation, quarantine, and exception handling
	•	policy-aware routing based on source and content profile

This layer is important because it ensures onboarding is repeatable, governed, and suitable for multiple source types.

It also provides the first decision point for handling content differently based on:
	•	sensitivity
	•	source trust
	•	residency
	•	legal/usage constraints
	•	intended processing path

⸻

6.3 Content Preparation & Normalisation

This is one of the most important layers in the architecture.

Its role is to transform heterogeneous source artefacts into a canonical, governed content form that can be used consistently by downstream services.

Depending on content type, this may include:
	•	format detection and modality routing
	•	document parsing and OCR
	•	speech-to-text and transcript handling
	•	thread and conversation normalisation
	•	cleaning, canonicalisation, and deduplication
	•	metadata extraction and enrichment
	•	classification, provenance capture, and redaction
	•	sensitive-data handling and content controls

The output of this layer is a canonical content object with the metadata and control attributes needed for downstream orchestration and retrieval.

⸻

6.4 Orchestration & Semantic Processing

This layer coordinates how content is processed once it has been normalised.

It supports:
	•	deterministic workflow orchestration
	•	processing rules and routing logic
	•	replay, retry, and controlled human intervention
	•	optional AI-enabled or semantic processing paths

This layer also includes stateful agent execution where appropriate.

Stateful agents should be understood as a specialised execution pattern for:
	•	long-running flows
	•	multi-step reasoning
	•	iterative processing
	•	governed tool use

They are not assumed to be the default for all workloads.

Semantic processing in this layer may include:
	•	chunking
	•	embeddings
	•	entity and relation extraction
	•	knowledge enrichment
	•	graph build
	•	indexing

These services should be viewed as composable shared capabilities, applied where the use case justifies them.

⸻

6.5 Governed Integration & AI Services

This layer supports controlled interaction between orchestration/runtime services and shared AI or enterprise tooling.

It includes:
	•	MCP-based tool access patterns
	•	governed mediation of service and tool calls
	•	context, authorisation, and trace propagation
	•	access to shared AI platform services such as LLMs, embeddings, moderation, and ML models

This layer is important because it ensures:
	•	AI workflows do not connect directly to tools in an unmanaged way
	•	access is policy-aware
	•	execution remains traceable
	•	model and tool interactions are governed consistently

MCP should be treated as a governed interaction pattern, not as the central integration mechanism for all data movement.

⸻

6.6 Persistence Services

This layer stores the outputs of the platform according to purpose.

It typically includes:
	•	raw content stores
	•	processed artefact stores
	•	metadata, lineage, and provenance stores
	•	analytical or relational stores
	•	vector stores
	•	graph stores
	•	session state and cache

This separation is intentional.

It avoids forcing all content into one store and allows the architecture to support:
	•	replay and audit
	•	semantic retrieval
	•	graph-driven knowledge use cases
	•	operational analytics
	•	stateful runtime behaviour

The persistence layer should also be read as logically separated from any one deployment environment. In practice, these stores may be:
	•	centralised
	•	cloud-local
	•	region-local
	•	on-prem
	•	or federated

depending on policy and platform needs.

⸻

6.7 Retrieval & Consumption

This layer makes the processed and governed content usable.

It supports:
	•	retrieval orchestration
	•	hybrid search
	•	policy and entitlement filtering
	•	content and retrieval APIs
	•	assistants and RAG experiences
	•	enterprise application integration
	•	downstream analytics and data consumption

A key architecture point is that retrieval is not just a technical search function. It must also include retrieval-time governance, such as:
	•	entitlement checks
	•	policy filtering
	•	provenance handling
	•	ranking and context controls

This is especially important where sensitive and less-sensitive content may coexist.

⸻

6.8 Cross-Cutting Security, Governance & Operations

This is the control plane that applies across all layers.

It includes:
	•	identity and access control
	•	policy enforcement and DLP
	•	metadata, lineage, provenance, and catalogue alignment
	•	audit, observability, and operational monitoring
	•	encryption and key management
	•	retention and deletion controls
	•	external source trust and legal/usage controls
	•	residency and sovereignty controls

This layer is critical because the platform must assume that unstructured content may contain:
	•	confidential content
	•	customer or employee data
	•	regulated material
	•	external content with lower trust or usage restrictions

The architecture therefore treats governance and security as first-class capabilities rather than downstream add-ons.

⸻

7. End-to-End Flow Summary

At a high level, the A1 architecture follows this sequence:
	1.	Content originates in one or more enterprise or external source domains.
	2.	It is onboarded into the platform through controlled ingestion and intake patterns.
	3.	It is prepared and normalised according to modality and content type.
	4.	It is processed through deterministic workflows and, where needed, stateful agent patterns and semantic services.
	5.	It interacts with AI services and tools through governed integration mechanisms.
	6.	It is stored in role-specific persistence services.
	7.	It is exposed through retrieval and API layers for assistants, applications, analytics, and downstream consumers.
	8.	Security, governance, lineage, and operational controls apply throughout the lifecycle.

